WEBVTT

0
00:00:03.839 --> 00:00:05.699
Welcome to module 2.

1
00:00:06.159 --> 00:00:07.590
For the last 10 years,

2
00:00:07.679 --> 00:00:10.100
cloud engineers have relied on a few

3
00:00:10.640 --> 00:00:14.500
well-established design principles to build highly available,

4
00:00:14.720 --> 00:00:16.020
scalable systems.

5
00:00:16.799 --> 00:00:20.069
But what happens when you take those proven rules

6
00:00:20.290 --> 00:00:23.840
and apply them to a massive AI training cluster?

7
00:00:24.170 --> 00:00:27.200
The system starts to behave in unexpected ways,

8
00:00:27.370 --> 00:00:28.799
and the worst part is,

9
00:00:28.969 --> 00:00:30.430
it doesn't break loudly,

10
00:00:30.549 --> 00:00:32.909
it breaks in ways that are hard to detect,

11
00:00:33.360 --> 00:00:34.270
diagnose,

12
00:00:34.409 --> 00:00:35.529
and reason about,

13
00:00:35.759 --> 00:00:38.909
often leading to underutilized compute capacity.

14
00:00:40.099 --> 00:00:43.360
These systems don't fail the way traditional systems fail.

15
00:00:44.430 --> 00:00:44.889
Today,

16
00:00:45.069 --> 00:00:48.810
we are gonna break down the hidden infrastructure assumptions

17
00:00:49.029 --> 00:00:52.209
that create these bottlenecks at AI scale.

18
00:00:52.509 --> 00:00:54.569
Let's look at assumption number one,

19
00:00:54.869 --> 00:00:56.830
the infinite compute illusion.

20
00:00:57.709 --> 00:00:59.830
In traditional cloud architecture,

21
00:01:00.009 --> 00:01:02.630
if a web application gets too much traffic,

22
00:01:02.869 --> 00:01:03.700
what do we do?

23
00:01:03.990 --> 00:01:06.410
We spin up more virtual machines.

24
00:01:06.870 --> 00:01:07.610
The assumption

25
00:01:07.870 --> 00:01:10.510
is that scaling compute horizontally

26
00:01:10.949 --> 00:01:13.769
provides a linear increase in performance.

27
00:01:14.610 --> 00:01:16.360
But in distributed AI

28
00:01:16.790 --> 00:01:19.010
compute does not scale independently.

29
00:01:19.660 --> 00:01:23.680
Imagine you're running a massive large language model training job.

30
00:01:23.930 --> 00:01:26.739
You throw 10,000 GPUs at the problem,

31
00:01:26.900 --> 00:01:28.559
but instead of going faster,

32
00:01:28.699 --> 00:01:30.690
your performance flatlines.

33
00:01:31.180 --> 00:01:31.650
Why?

34
00:01:32.059 --> 00:01:36.440
Because AI compute is driven by coordination across the network.

35
00:01:37.300 --> 00:01:41.569
If your interconnects can't feed data to those GPUs fast enough,

36
00:01:42.029 --> 00:01:45.489
you just bought the world's most expensive space heaters.

37
00:01:47.019 --> 00:01:48.230
Assumption number 2,

38
00:01:48.430 --> 00:01:51.010
the independent stateless node fallacy.

39
00:01:52.540 --> 00:01:55.300
Traditional cloud thrives on statelessness.

40
00:01:56.989 --> 00:01:58.760
If server A crashes,

41
00:01:59.059 --> 00:02:03.010
the load balancer simply routes the traffic to server B.

42
00:02:03.430 --> 00:02:04.849
The system survives

43
00:02:05.190 --> 00:02:07.809
because the nodes don't depend on each other.

44
00:02:08.589 --> 00:02:12.210
Distributed AI systems are the exact opposite.

45
00:02:12.509 --> 00:02:14.070
They are highly stateful,

46
00:02:14.210 --> 00:02:15.389
tightly coupled,

47
00:02:15.589 --> 00:02:17.330
and tightly coordinated.

48
00:02:18.029 --> 00:02:19.710
During an AI training run,

49
00:02:20.100 --> 00:02:23.550
nodes must continuously exchange updates to

50
00:02:23.550 --> 00:02:26.490
maintain consistency across the cluster.

51
00:02:27.380 --> 00:02:30.559
Here is a concrete example of how this breaks down.

52
00:02:31.300 --> 00:02:37.940
If one single GPU out of 1000 experiences a microsecond delay or drops a packet,

53
00:02:38.229 --> 00:02:40.770
the other 999 GPUs

54
00:02:41.029 --> 00:02:43.619
often cannot make progress efficiently.

55
00:02:43.910 --> 00:02:47.570
They must sit idle and wait for the lagging node to catch up.

56
00:02:48.399 --> 00:02:49.350
In the cloud,

57
00:02:49.600 --> 00:02:52.240
a localized failure is isolated.

58
00:02:52.399 --> 00:02:53.220
In AI,

59
00:02:53.520 --> 00:02:55.059
a localized failure

60
00:02:55.199 --> 00:02:58.830
can propagate rapidly across the entire cluster,

61
00:02:59.160 --> 00:03:01.470
which brings us to assumption number 3,

62
00:03:01.639 --> 00:03:04.449
latency as a best effort metric.

63
00:03:05.949 --> 00:03:07.929
In a standard web environment,

64
00:03:08.119 --> 00:03:11.330
an extra 50 milliseconds of network latency

65
00:03:11.630 --> 00:03:13.750
goes completely unnoticed by a user.

66
00:03:14.619 --> 00:03:16.059
In an AI cluster,

67
00:03:16.229 --> 00:03:19.179
latency is not just a user experience issue,

68
00:03:19.350 --> 00:03:23.210
it is a critical system constraint for maintaining coherence.

69
00:03:24.119 --> 00:03:29.119
When you are coordinating massive gradient updates across data center halls,

70
00:03:29.559 --> 00:03:31.520
small variations in latency

71
00:03:31.759 --> 00:03:33.660
doesn't just slow things down,

72
00:03:33.919 --> 00:03:37.160
it can significantly reduce your scale efficiency.

73
00:03:38.070 --> 00:03:40.289
The network is no longer just a pipe,

74
00:03:40.589 --> 00:03:43.559
it is the actual backplane of the computer.

75
00:03:44.509 --> 00:03:48.949
If you try to architect an AI system using cloud native instincts,

76
00:03:49.059 --> 00:03:54.059
focusing only on raw compute power while treating the network as an afterthought,

77
00:03:54.389 --> 00:03:56.949
you quickly run into scaling limits.

78
00:03:58.270 --> 00:03:59.520
To build for AI

79
00:03:59.919 --> 00:04:03.960
we have to move beyond designing for independent stateless systems

80
00:04:04.240 --> 00:04:08.449
and start designing for large scale coordinated system behavior.

81
00:04:09.130 --> 00:04:10.479
In our next lesson,

82
00:04:10.690 --> 00:04:14.550
we'll look at how this directly changes the physical architecture

83
00:04:14.970 --> 00:04:16.829
between transactional systems

84
00:04:17.089 --> 00:04:18.510
and AI systems.